// THE DEEP-HANDLER LAW at the ARM: an arm that performs the very op it
// handles must resolve OUTWARD — to the next install down the chain — never
// back into itself. `inner`'s arm performs `note`, so under the law that
// reaches `outer` (5 * 10 = 50), and the arm adds 1: 51. Under a
// perform-site-world dispatch it reaches `inner` again and recurses forever.
//
// BORN RED 2026-09-09, answering 52 — one self-dispatch before it escapes.
// The law is not a reading of the literature: the evidence tier's own emit
// comment states it, "the arm's own performs resolve outer, never self".
//
// THE TRACE, and it is a divergence between two implementations of one walk.
// main's `note(5)` takes the LEXICAL tier, which emits a bare direct call
// with NO world bracket, so `inner`'s arm runs with the world still
// containing `inner`. The arm's own `note(n)` takes the EVIDENCE tier, walks
// that un-narrowed world, and finds `inner` — self. THAT call brackets
// correctly (evidence sets the world to the node's install_world), so the
// second arm reaches `outer` and returns 51, and the first arm adds its own
// 1: 52. Exactly one extra hop, which is what one missing bracket buys.
//
// The install already saves what the fix needs — `_wprev`, the chain from
// before its own push, which its comment calls "what the arm dispatch sets
// around the arm call". The lexical tier never reads it.
// Hβ.effects.lexical-tier-omits-the-world-bracket carries it.
//
// RECORDED AS A CORRECTION: an earlier read of this fixture called the
// hypothesis refuted, on the strength of a 501 that was never measured —
// the multiplier was 100, the exit was out of range, and 501 was INFERRED
// from a runtime error message that carried no number. 502 would have
// produced the identical message. Expected when fixed: 51.
effect Log { note(n: Int) -> Int }

handler outer {
  note(n) => resume(n * 10),
}

handler inner {
  note(n) => resume(note(n) + 1),
}

fn main() = note(5) ~> inner ~> outer
